# React render safety

Treat every state update in an effect as a possible render loop.

## Required checks

- Effects synchronize with external systems; derive render values directly or with `useMemo`.
- Never put an inline parent callback in a child's effect dependency list. Stabilize it with
  `useCallback`, preferably using functional state updates.
- An effect that sets state must compare old and new values and skip equivalent updates.
- Do not copy props into state unless the user can edit the copy independently and prop changes
  have an explicit synchronization policy.
- Memoized callbacks include every value they read. Do not suppress `exhaustive-deps`.
- Avoid setting a new object/array when its semantic value did not change.

```tsx
// Bad: callback identity changes and the child effect calls it again.
<ConfigForm onChange={(patch) => setConfig({ ...config, ...patch })} />

// Good: stable identity and no stale closure.
const handleConfigChange = useCallback((patch: object) => {
  setConfig((previous) => ({ ...previous, ...patch }));
}, []);
```

## Verification

- Add a StrictMode-capable mount test for forms with effects and parent callbacks.
- Assert the component settles without “Maximum update depth exceeded”.
- Run focused ESLint hook checks; fix warnings instead of disabling them.
